but then maybe just by chance x2 y2 is just a harder mini batch.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:06.700
Maybe even some mislabeled examples in it in which case the cost would be a bit higher and so on.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:12.820
So that's why you get these oscillations as you plot the cost when you're running mini batch gradient descent.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:17.180
Now one of the parameters you need to choose is the size of your mini batch.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:27.620
So M was the training set size. On one extreme, if the mini batch size is equal to M,

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:38.690
then you just end up with batch gradient descent. So in this extreme, you would just have one mini batch,

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:45.010
x1 y1, and this mini batch is equal to your entire training set.

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:49.210
So setting the mini batch size to M just gives you back gradient descent.

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:57.460
The other extreme would be if your mini batch size were equal to 1.

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:04.230
This gives you an algorithm called stochastic gradient descent.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:11.530
And here, every example is its own mini batch.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:22.690
So what you do in this case is you look at the first mini batch.

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:31.290
So x1 y1. But when your mini batch size is 1, this just has your first training example.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:34.250
And you take your gradient descent step with your first training example.

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:41.010
And then you next take a look at your second mini batch, which is just your second training example,

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:42.890
and take a gradient descent step with that.

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:48.010
And then you do it with the third training example and so on, looking at just one single training example at a time.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:55.780
So let's look at what these two extremes will do on optimizing this cost function.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:04:00.700
If these are the contours of a cost function you're trying to minimise, so your minimum is there,

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:09.420
then batch gradient descent might start somewhere and be able to take relatively low noise,

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:15.140
relatively large steps and just keep marching to the minimum.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:21.540
In contrast, with stochastic gradient descent, if you start somewhere, let's pick a different starting point,

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:26.780
then on every iteration, you're taking gradient descent with just a single training example.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:32.460
So most of the time you hit toward the global minimum, but sometimes you hit in the wrong direction.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:36.180
If that one example happens to point you in a bad direction.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:43.100
So stochastic gradient descent can be extremely noisy and on average, it'll take you in a good direction,

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:47.140
but sometimes it'll hit in the wrong direction as well.

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:50.300
And stochastic gradient descent won't ever converge.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:54.740
You'll always just kind of oscillate and wander around the region of the minimum,

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:57.380
but it won't ever just head to the minimum and stay there.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:10.330
In practice, the mini batch size you use will be somewhere in between, somewhere between in one and M.

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:14.530
And one and M are respectively too small and too large.

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:15.930
And here's why.

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:27.180
If you use batch gradient descent, so this is your mini batch size equals M,

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:35.180
then you're processing a huge training set on every iteration.

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:41.660
So the main disadvantage of this is that it takes too much time, too long per iteration.

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:46.700
Assuming you have a very large training set, if you have a small training set, then batch gradient descent is fine.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:59.910
If you go to the opposite, if you use stochastic gradient descent, then it's nice that you get to make progress after processing just one example.

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:01.910
That's actually not a problem.

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:07.190
And the noisiness can be ameliorated or can be reduced by just using a smaller learning rate.

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:17.190
But the huge disadvantage of stochastic gradient descent is that you lose almost all your speed up from vectorization.

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:21.550
Because here you're processing a single training example at a time.

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:25.750
The way you process each example is going to be very inefficient.

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:40.220
So what works best in practice is something in between where you have some, you know, mini batch size that's not too big or too small.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:48.820
And this gives you, in practice, the fastest learning.

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:54.630
And you notice that this has two good things going for it.

00:06:54.670 --> 00:06:57.550
One is that you do get a lot of vectorization.

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:08.470
So in the example we used on the previous video, if your mini batch size was a thousand examples, then, you know, you might be able to vectorize across a thousand examples,

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:12.230
which is going to be much faster than processing the examples one at a time.

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:27.680
And second, you can also make progress without needing to wait till you process the entire training set.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:40.260
So again, using the numbers we had from the previous video, each epoch or each pass through your training set allows you to say 5,000 gradient descent steps.

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:45.580
So in practice, there'll be some in between mini batch size that works best.

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:50.700
And so with mini batch in the end, if you start here, maybe one iteration does this.

00:07:51.060 --> 00:08:03.220
Two iterations, three, four, you know, and it's not guaranteed to always head toward the minimum, but it tends to head more consistently and directionally minimum than stochastic gradient descent.

